## Evidence (D-088, `stage2-dotnet-clientserver-second-poller-removal-summary.md` BLOCKERS)
Public API repro: tcp ROUTER (REJECT default) + DEALER; first request OK; DEALER `disconnect(endpoint)`, observe that endpoint's
Disconnected on the DEALER monitor, `connect(endpoint)`, wait for the READY edge, submit one request → the server never receives it
and the request times out (1 s). Cause: the ROUTER still holds the old pipe (its termination not yet processed) and rejects the
new same-RID pipe without closing it, so the DEALER stays on a pipe that can never carry traffic.

## What to do
1. In ROUTER admission (`router_admission.cpp` REJECT branch of identify_peer): keep the existing pipe, do NOT register the duplicate,
   and close the duplicate pipe immediately (orderly, through the existing pipe termination path so the connector gets
   Disconnected/Closed and its reconnect intent re-arms). Pending REQUESTs pinned to that pipe complete once with
   REQUEST_NOT_CONNECTED via the same terminal helper used by 8b82d51b75 (no second bookkeeping).
2. Contract test (tcp + inproc): the D-088 sequence — after the old pipe terminates, the connector's next attempt is admitted and
   a request succeeds; a request submitted on the rejected pipe completes NOT_CONNECTED within ~100 ms, never a timeout; existing
   REJECT tests (`test_router_handover` etc.) stay green. 20 reps.
3. Full `ctest -j2` + §5 checks (hotpath check if `scripts/` provides one; report numbers).
